Correct
A solution architect wants to minimize custom development and use native platform capability wherever it cleanly satisfies the requirement.



Which two requirements can be met with out-of-the-box functionality in Microsoft Power Platform? (Select TWO.)

A. Synchronous REST validation on save

Your selection is correct
B. Business process flow

C. Embedded external chart library

D. 15-second ERP recalculation loop

Your selection is correct
E. Approval flow

Overall explanation
Business process flows are built to guide users through staged business processes in a consistent step-by-step experience on records. Approval flows are also a native pattern in Power Automate, where built-in approval actions can handle human sign-off scenarios without requiring a custom approval engine.



The key architectural skill here is recognizing where the platform already has a first-class capability and where a requirement crosses into extension territory. Guided stages on records and routed approvals are strong examples of native functionality, while synchronous external validation, embedded external visualization logic, and tight polling-style recalculation usually push the design toward custom integration or code.



Why the other options are incorrect:

• A. Synchronous REST validation on save. This requirement implies a real-time external call in the transaction path, which is not a simple out-of-the-box design choice. Even if you can build integrations in Power Platform, this specific pattern moves into custom integration behavior rather than a native declarative feature. It is plausible because integration is common in PL-400 solutions, but it is not the clean out-of-the-box answer here.



• C. Embedded external chart library. Power Platform offers native controls and visualization options, but embedding a third-party charting library is not the same as using out-of-the-box platform functionality. This choice introduces a custom presentation component and its lifecycle considerations. It sounds attractive because it is still UI-oriented, but it exceeds the native capability boundary implied by the question.



• D. 15-second ERP recalculation loop. This describes a near-real-time cross-system polling pattern, not a standard out-of-the-box feature choice. It would raise architecture questions around frequency, latency, dependency management, and integration design. The requirement is therefore much closer to custom orchestration or code than to a native feature switch.

