Question 6
Correct
A developer must prevent users from creating a Dataverse account record when a required tax registration value is missing. The validation must run on the server, apply regardless of which app or integration creates the record, and reject the request before the main database transaction whenever possible.



The developer should register a ____.

Your answer is correct
A. Synchronous PreValidation plug-in

Explanation
A Synchronous PreValidation plug-in is the correct choice because it allows the developer to run validation logic on the server side before the main transaction is executed. This type of plug-in ensures that the validation is enforced regardless of the source of the record creation and can reject the request before it reaches the main database transaction.
B. Asynchronous PostOperation plug-in

Explanation
An Asynchronous PostOperation plug-in is not the best choice for this scenario because it runs after the main transaction has been completed. This means that the record would already be created in the database, potentially violating the required tax registration value constraint.
C. Client-side business rule

Explanation
A Client-side business rule is not suitable for this scenario because it runs on the client side, meaning it would not be able to enforce the validation requirement on the server side. This type of rule is more focused on user interface validation and may not prevent the creation of the record at the database level.
D. Power Automate cloud flow approval

Explanation
A Power Automate cloud flow approval is not the appropriate choice for this scenario as it is used for automating approval processes and workflows, rather than enforcing data validation rules. This type of flow would not be able to prevent the creation of a record based on a missing tax registration value.
Overall explanation
Synchronous PreValidation plug-in is the correct answer because it can run server-side before the main Dataverse database transaction. This makes it appropriate for validation logic that must block an invalid create request before the platform commits the operation.



This is a 9/10 PL-400 distinction because several Power Platform tools can apply business logic, but they do not run at the same point or with the same enforcement strength. For server-side validation that must apply consistently across apps, APIs, imports, and integrations, a Dataverse plug-in registered in the appropriate event pipeline stage is the strongest fit.



Why the other options are incorrect:



• B. Asynchronous PostOperation plug-in is incorrect. A PostOperation plug-in runs after the core operation, and asynchronous execution occurs outside the immediate transaction path. That makes it unsuitable for blocking the original create request before it is committed. It is useful for follow-up processing, integration, or non-blocking work, but not for immediate validation that must reject the transaction.



• C. Client-side business rule is incorrect. A business rule can guide user input in model-driven apps and may enforce some logic at the form level. However, client-side logic can be bypassed by integrations, imports, API calls, or other clients. The requirement says the validation must run regardless of which app or integration creates the record, so the logic must be enforced server-side.



• D. Power Automate cloud flow approval is incorrect. A cloud flow can automate follow-up tasks and approvals, but it is not the best mechanism for synchronous server-side rejection in the Dataverse event pipeline. It would introduce extra process overhead and would not naturally prevent the original database operation before the main transaction. This option confuses workflow automation with transactional validation logic.

