A model-driven app uses a field code component on the Case table. The component updates one existing text column on cases that the signed-in user already owns.



Which privilege combination should the user's Dataverse security role include?



Select only one answer.

A. Case Create, Write, Delete

Explanation
The user does not need the privilege to create or delete cases in this scenario. The only required privileges are to read and update the existing text column on cases that the user already owns.
B. Case Read, Append, Assign

Explanation
The user does not need the privilege to append or assign cases in this scenario. The only required privileges are to read and update the existing text column on cases that the user already owns.
Correct answer
C. Case Read, Write

Explanation
The user needs to have the privilege to read the cases to access the existing text column and the privilege to write to update the text column. This combination of privileges allows the user to view and modify the necessary data.
Your answer is incorrect
D. Case Read, Write, Share, Append To

Explanation
The user does not need the additional privileges to share or append to cases in this scenario. The only required privileges are to read and update the existing text column on cases that the user already owns.
Overall explanation
A field code component that edits an existing Case column needs the same core Dataverse privileges the user needs to open the record and save the change. In Dataverse, security roles define what operations users can perform on a table, and Read plus Write is the minimal combination for this scenario. That is the cleanest least-privilege fit because the component is not creating, deleting, assigning, sharing, or relating records.



Least privilege means granting only the operations required for the business task and nothing broader. Microsoft’s security guidance emphasizes minimum required access, and Dataverse role design is built around assigning the specific privileges needed for the app behavior. Here, the component updates an existing value on a record the user already owns, so Case Read and Case Write are sufficient.



Why the other options are incorrect:

• A. Create and Delete are unnecessary because the component is not creating new Case rows or removing existing ones. Adding those privileges broadens the user’s effective permissions beyond the stated requirement. That violates least-privilege design even though the component might still function.

• B. Append and Assign do not solve the core need to edit the existing Case column. Without Write, the user still cannot save the updated value back to the record. This option adds unrelated privileges while omitting the one privilege that actually enables the update.

• D. This would work, but it grants more access than required because Share and Append To are not needed for a simple in-place column update. Least privilege is not just about making the component run; it is about avoiding extra rights that expand the user’s operational surface. A broader role is therefore the wrong design choice here.

